  "preamble": "The glottal fricative [h] is replaced by the velar fricative [x] when speakers of a language that has /x/ but no /h/ take over words containing [h]. In Spanish, words that kept the Old Spanish aspirate (from Latin f- or from foreign h) entered the standard through Andalusian speech, where the aspirate had merged with jota as [h]; Castilian speakers then mapped it onto their /x/ (jolgorio, juerga beside huelga, jaca < haca), and English h is regularly adapted the same way (jonrón 'home run'). Russian, Greek and Serbo-Croatian, which also lack /h/, render English or German [h] as [x] (хобби, χόμπι, hobi). As a native sound change, Guaymí (Ngäbere, Chibchan) turned word-initial Proto-Chibchan *h into x, as in /xu/ 'house'.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Fortition, Buccalization, Merger",
